{"attributes": {"type": "DifferentialExperiment", "particle": "decay", "reaction": "PFNS", "nuclide": "Cf-252", "library": "ARIADNE", "documentation": "EXFOR No. = 22202.003+22202.004+22202.005. Journal article = Chalupka et al., Nuclear Science and Engineering, Vol.106, Issue.3, p.367 (1990).The data by Chalupka et al. are in EXFOR sub-entries 22202.003 (9--12.76 MeV), 22202.004 (13.98--27.10 MeV), 22202.005 (9--15.52 MeV), 22202.006 (9--18.94 MeV), and 22202.007 (9.99--20.02 MeV). The different EXFOR entries correspond to different energy ranges or bias settings (2.0, and 2.75 MeV). They can be combined considering correlations if not the same or overlapping energy ranges are used; otherwise, one would double count data as they stem from the same measurement. The journal publications were published after Mannhart\u2019s evaluation, but he considered these data along with those of Maerten et al. for inclusion.The main aim of this measurement was to counter-check the upturn of the PFNS above 20 MeV seen by the Maerten et al. group which was contrary to the evaluation by Mannhart. They identify as main potential issue in that measurement above 20 MeV background from cosmic radiation. In order to reduce this specific background, Chalupka et al. performed their measurement in a mine in Bad Bleiberg, Austria which is 1000 m below a mountain known as a rich harvesting grounds for lead and tin. In essence, they had a 1000-m thick natural Pb shield to reduce random-coincidence background. One 25.4x5.08-cm scintillation detector of type NE213 was used for all runs to count neutrons. The efficiency of this detector was low (20%). The efficiency values were calculated by the MC technique using NEFF-4 up to 20 MeV. For 18-29 MeV, they used the 1H cross section data adjusted to NEFF results between 16-18 MeV, which agree well with today's JENDL-5.0 data. The light output curves were calibrated with 22Na, 60Co, and 208Tl Compton edge energies The TOF path was 279.6 cm long and had uncertainty of 2 mm. The neutron detector was located at 0 degree relative to the perpendicular plane of the 252Cf deposit. Fission fragments were detected in an  99.5\\% efficient fission chamber. The 252Cf deposit was a layer of 15 mm diameter on platinum backing with 15 mm diameter; it had an activity of 1.5643x10e5 f/s. The time resolution of the detection system was 1.0 ns FWHM. It could be that the detectors used were those used for the measurement of Boettger et al.Corrections were made for all known effects, and detailed partial uncertainties were provided for all uncertainties either explicitly or stating that they were 0.There were some minor concerns: One was that the carbon cross section used in NEFF-4 agreed with JENDL-5.0 only to 7% (total) and 9% (inleastic), however, the influence should be small as the main cross section used if H. Also, it was not completely clear in how far the 252Cf PFNS spectrum was used for the detector response. The authors of this data set could, unfortunately, no clarify this anymore. As these are minor concerns, and many important corrections were performed and detailed uncertainties are provided, we accept the data until 25 MeV, which is the limit of our evaluation.\n        ", "date": "2023-07-05", "Author": ["A.Chalupka", "L.Malek", "S.Tagesen", "R.Boettger"], "Year": "1990", "Facility": "N/A", "Institute": ["2AUSIRK", "2GERPTB"], "Isotope": "Cf-252", "Quantity": "PFNS", "MeasurementType": "PFNS", "ConcernEXFOR": "N/A", "ConcernAIACHNE": ["Response function", "Multiple scattering"], "ConcernAIACHNE_text": "We are not sure how Maxwellian of T=1.42 was used to get the detector response. There could be a circular argument there. There could be scattering from the shadow cone leading to neutrons from water and iron. Gammas will have been taken care of by PSD cuts.", "BackgroundCorrected": "y", "AlphaBackground": "y", "GammaBackground": "y", "RandomCoincidenceBackground": "y", "ShadowBarBackground": "yes", "WrapAroundBackground": "N/A", "MultipleScatteringSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "MultipleScatteringSurroundingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSurroundingCorrected": "y", "FissionDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionResponseCorrected": "y", "SampleDecayCorrected": "unknown", "FissionFragmentAbsorptioninSampleCorrected": "y", "SignalPulsePileupCorrected": "y", "DeadtimeCorrected": "y", "AngularDistributionFissionFragmentsCorrected": "y", "ImpuritiesCorrected": "y", "Mannhart": "no, but strongly recommended", "CorrelatedData": ["31854002", "31854003", "30969002", "30969003"], "EXFORnumber": ["22202003", "22202004", "22202005", "22202006", "22202007"], "Accepted": "y", "MissingCorrections": "multiple scattering", "FissionDetector": {"Type": "FISCH", "ParticleDetected": "FF", "Efficiency": 99.5, "EfficiencyMethod": ["Calculated", "STAPRE"], "FFAbsorptionAngularDistributionMethod": ["Calculated", "STAPRE"], "AngularAcceptaneofFF": "> 5 degree", "Gas": {"Type": "Methane", "Purity": 100.0, "Pressure": "unknown"}, "Size": {"geometry": "unknown"}, "NoPhotomultipliers": "unknown", "Weight": "15 g", "StructuralMaterial": "unknown", "ReferenceDetector": "N/A"}, "NeutronDetector": {"Type": "SCIN (liquid)", "Number": "NE213", "ParticleDetected": "N", "Efficiency": 20.0, "ResponseDeterminationMethod": ["Calculated by MC", "NEFF4"], "EfficiencyDeterminationMethod": ["Calculated by MC", "NEFF4"], "Angle": 0.0, "TOFLength": {"Value": 2.796, "Unit": "m", "Unc": 2.0, "Unit_Unc": "mm"}, "trsl": {"Value": 1.0, "Unit": "ns", "Measure": "FWHM"}, "t0Determination": "Gamma Peak", "Gas": {"Type": "unknown", "Purity": "unknown", "Pressure": "unknown"}, "Size": {"geometry": "cylinder", "diameter": "25.4 cm", "height": "5.08 cm"}, "NoPhotomultipliers": 1, "Photomultipliers": "XP2041", "Weight": "unknown", "StructuralMaterial": "unknown", "ReferenceDetector": "None", "ReferenceReaction": "(n-p) scattering"}, "Shield